<h2>The pricing question</h2>

<p>At $249 per month as a starting point, Keap is more than double the cost of DUSA before you factor in add-ons, additional users, or growing contact lists. And because Keap does not include a funnel builder or review management, many businesses end up paying for additional tools on top of that base price.</p>

<p>DUSA includes all of those features in a single plan at a fraction of the cost. For a small business owner comparing the two, the question becomes: what are you getting for that extra $150+ per month? In most cases, the answer is more complex automation tooling that you may never need.</p>

<p>It is also worth noting that Keap's pricing increases as your contact list grows. DUSA does not penalise you for building a bigger database. Your pricing stays predictable regardless of how many contacts you add.</p>

<h2>When Keap is the better choice</h2>

<p>Keap is the better choice if your business relies on deeply complex, multi-branch automation workflows. If you need decision trees with dozens of conditional paths, advanced lead scoring with automated routing, and campaign logic that mirrors a flowchart spanning an entire wall, Keap was built for that level of complexity.</p>

<p>It is also a reasonable option if you already have a team member or consultant who knows the platform well. For businesses that have invested in Keap expertise and have workflows already running, switching platforms has a real cost. If Keap is working for you today, there may not be a compelling reason to change.</p>

<h2>When DUSA is the better choice</h2>

<p>DUSA is the better choice if you are a sole operator or small team that wants powerful automation without needing a certification to use it. If you want CRM, email, SMS, funnels, review management, and appointment booking in one place, at a price that does not require enterprise revenue to justify, DUSA is built for that reality.</p>

<p>It is also the better choice if you value speed of setup. Most DUSA users are operational within their first week. With Keap, that timeline is typically measured in weeks, and often requires paid onboarding or consulting to get right.</p>

<blockquote><p>The best automation platform is the one your team will actually use. Power that sits behind a steep learning curve is power you are paying for but never accessing.</p></blockquote>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>

<p>Keap and DUSA both aim to help small businesses automate their marketing, manage their contacts, and grow their revenue. Keap approaches the problem with deep automation tooling and a price tag to match. DUSA approaches it by giving small business owners everything they need in one platform, at a price that makes sense for their scale.</p>

<p>If you need enterprise-grade branching logic and you have the budget and expertise to use it, Keap has earned its place. But for the majority of small businesses, the combination of features, simplicity, and pricing that DUSA offers is the more practical path to growth.</p>

<p>Neither platform is the wrong choice in every situation. But if you are a small business owner looking for a tool you can actually set up, use, and afford, DUSA was built with you in mind.</p>

